        {
          "type": "text",
          "delta": "**What I changed**\n\n- Called `set_status` with `Ready`, moving the Demo orchard project from Paused \u2192 Ready. The tool returned \"Status set to Ready.\"\n- Then called `capture_panel` to capture the window. I did **not** touch Deploy or Cancel, and did not deploy anything.\n\n**What the screenshot shows**\n\n- `Project: Demo orchard` \u2014 correct project.\n- `Status: Ready` \u2014 the change took effect in the UI, not just the tool response.\n- `[ Deploy ]` and `[ Cancel ]` \u2014 both controls are still present and visible.\n- `Last check: All 12 checks passed` \u2014 the panel reports 12/12 checks passing.\n\nSo all four requested conditions are satisfied in the captured frame: Ready status, 12/12 checks, and both Deploy and Cancel controls still rendered.\n\n**Caveats worth flagging**\n\n- The screenshot establishes what the **panel displays**, not an independent verification of anything. \"All 12 checks passed\" is a line of text the panel renders; I have no way from this tooling to confirm the checks actually ran, what they tested, or when the \"last check\" occurred. Treat it as a UI claim, not audited evidence.\n- Likewise, the presence of a Deploy button only confirms the control is rendered \u2014 it says nothing about whether deployment is actually safe or advisable for this project.\n- I did not click Deploy, so no release was triggered. If you want the release actually shipped, that's a separate, explicit action I'd need you to authorize.\n\nOne inconsistency to note: the panel has no header showing an explicit prior state, so \"Paused \u2192 Ready\" is based on your instruction and the tool's confirmation, not something visible in the frame itself."
        },
